Tuples are the first data type that we're going to talk about.

00:58.760 --> 01:05.200
They're lightweight collections that are used to keep track of related but different items.

01:05.200 --> 01:10.040
There's no rule or syntax error that says they have to be used this way.

01:10.040 --> 01:16.800
lists are generally used to have contained similar types of items in them.

01:16.800 --> 01:20.800
But again, that's just a guideline, and you can use them however you want.

01:20.800 --> 01:25.680
The difference with tuples and lists is that tuples are immutable.

01:25.680 --> 01:29.320
Once a tuple is created, you cannot change the items in it.

01:29.320 --> 01:32.080
You cannot append, you cannot pop.

01:32.080 --> 01:37.400
You cannot do an assignment at an index, like you could with a list.

01:37.400 --> 01:46.760
But tuples are sorted, so they have an order to them, and how they do it.

01:46.760 --> 01:55.040
They're stored, so you can access these 0th item in a tuple and the first and so on and so forth.

01:55.040 --> 02:01.800
There are occasions where you expect an immutable type, so you don't want to pass a list into a function

02:01.800 --> 02:05.920
because it might get changed by something later down the line.

02:05.920 --> 02:16.720
And so tuple is a great way of ensuring that the data structure that you are passing around won't change.

02:16.720 --> 02:24.960
One of the good uses for tuple is storing information in a row in a spreadsheet.

02:24.960 --> 02:34.400
So a spreadsheet might have different values in different columns, and the tuple can be used to represent that.

